# Line endings for the generated content documents.
#
# `src/data/content/*.v1.json` are OUTPUTS of `npm run content:export`
# (scripts/exportEnemies.ts, scripts/exportModifiers.ts). Those exporters
# build the document text in memory joined with `\n` and write it verbatim,
# and their idempotency tests — tests/data/enemiesExportIdempotency.test.ts
# and tests/data/modifiersExportIdempotency.test.ts — compare the
# regenerated string against the committed file BYTE-FOR-BYTE. The byte
# comparison is the whole point of those tests: values can match while bytes
# differ (skills.v1.json shipped exactly that drift once, with non-ASCII
# escaping written raw instead of `\uXXXX`), so they must not be softened
# into a line-ending-tolerant compare.
#
# On a Windows clone with `core.autocrlf=true`, git rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. `readFileSync(..., 'utf8')` then returns CRLF while the exporter
# still produces LF, and the comparison fails on every line of the document.
# Pinning these files to LF in the working tree makes the exporter and the
# checkout agree on every platform, so the tests behave the same on Windows
# as they already do on Linux/macOS.
#
# Deliberately scoped to these generated documents ONLY. A repo-wide
# `* text=auto eol=lf` would rewrite ~418 other tracked text files on the
# next checkout — that is a separate decision, not this one. Tracked art
# under public/ and art-src/ is binary by git's own NUL-byte heuristic and
# is unaffected either way.
/src/data/content/*.v1.json text eol=lf

# Same disease, different symptom: the engine outcome baseline.
#
# `tests/engine/fixtures/outcomeBaseline.json` is written by
# `tests/engine/fixtures/captureOutcomeBaseline.ts`, which ends with
# `writeFileSync(OUT, JSON.stringify(...) + '\n')` — pure LF, on every
# platform. With `core.autocrlf=true` the checkout is CRLF, so regenerating
# on Windows rewrites ALL ~2000 lines. Nothing breaks (`JSON.parse` does not
# care), which is precisely why this rots unseen: the test stays green while
# the REVIEW signal is destroyed. That script's whole promise is "a per-case
# diff against the existing fixture so the blast radius is visible", and a
# whole-file diff hides the handful of hashes that actually moved behind
# 2000 lines that only changed their line endings.
/tests/engine/fixtures/*.json text eol=lf
